 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Smooth Subtraction](1840-smooth-subtraction.md)
-Smooth Subtraction provides a smooth approximation of the boolean difference operation ($A \setminus B$) for two Signed Distance Functions (SDFs). It blends the subtraction of one shape from another using a smoothing parameter $k$.
+Provides a differentiable approximation of the CSG difference by blending the subtraction with a smoothing parameter, avoiding sharp edges. Choose this when gradient-based optimization or avoiding artifacts in differentiable rendering is required.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per evaluation, requiring two SDF queries and basic arithmetic [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, no additional storage required [G].
-- **Measured Performance and Scaling:** Not given in the sources [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, efficient, allows for arbitrary complex shapes to be carved out of others [G].
-- **Limitations:** The resulting SDF is only valid if the input SDFs are valid. Sharp corners at the intersection of the base and subtractor may require careful normal estimation for rendering [G].
-- **When to use:** When you need to create holes, cutouts, or complex boolean combinations in SDF-based rendering [G].
-- **When not to use:** When smooth transitions between shapes are required (use Smooth Subtraction instead) [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample, requiring two SDF evaluations and one max operation.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory.
+- **Strengths:** Exact boolean representation; sharp, clean edges; computationally cheap.
+- **Limitations:** Non-differentiable at the cut boundary; can produce artifacts in differentiable rendering pipelines; sensitive to the alignment of the two shapes.
+- **When to use:** When hard, precise boolean cuts are required and differentiability is not needed.
+- **When not to use:** When smooth transitions or gradient-based optimization are required (use [Smooth Subtraction](1840-smooth-subtraction.md) instead).
